  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "7QmQr5Fqj057uBgvtT9iqKg0SnM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-16T20:32:31.340Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Claim\n\nWhen skill invocations are recorded only as tool calls and log lines, the downstream artifact carries no structured trace of which skill produced which fragment under whose authority.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout that trace, evaluation collapses to output scoring, safety auditing has only the prompt and final string to inspect, and knowledge claims become unfalsifiable\u2014even a true answer cannot be shown to come from a reliable process.\n\n## Evidence from source\n\nThe paper frames this as the missing fourth question in the Agent Skills literature: not only how to design, evaluate, and secure skills, but how to *record what a skill did*.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-16T20:32:31.340Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-16T20:32:31.340Z"
    }
  },
