  {
    "index": 10,
    "hash": "y4PWRIq9YONrniZHUIoZsRkqoH8=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T10:03:30.116Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Claim\n\nWhen skill invocations are recorded only as tool calls and log lines, the downstream artifact carries no structured trace of which skill produced which fragment under whose authority.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout that trace, evaluation collapses to output scoring, safety auditing has only the prompt and final string to inspect, and knowledge claims become unfalsifiable\u2014even a true answer cannot be shown to come from a reliable process.\n\n## Evidence from source\n\nThe paper frames this as the missing fourth question in the Agent Skills literature: not only how to design, evaluate, and secure skills, but how to *record what a skill did*.\n\n## Methodology grounding\nThe claims below live in the plugin's `data/methodology/` corpus; the relationship is stated per claim.\n\n- **[[agents are simultaneously methodology executors and subjects creating a unique trust asymmetry]]** \u2014 BUILDS_ON. Provenance blindness is obliviousness seen from the outside: the methodology claim borrows Kiczales' term for base code that cannot observe the aspects modifying it, and a tool call is exactly a transformation whose provenance the calling agent cannot see.\n- **[[prospective memory requires externalization]]** \u2014 RELATES_TO. A tool call leaves nothing for a later session to read, so any obligation it created is lost at the session boundary \u2014 the failure mode that claim generalises.\n",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T10:03:30.116Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T10:03:30.116Z"
    }
  }
